 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of splat fragments. Each fragment performs a constant number of arithmetic operations and texture samples [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of splat fragments [G]. Each fragment performs a constant number of arithmetic operations and texture samples [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ for the G-Buffer textures, where $W$ and $H$ are the viewport dimensions [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Explicit control over occlusion and weighting allows for high-quality rendering of translucent surfaces. Decoupling attributes from the final color buffer enables deferred shading techniques [G].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a pre-populated depth buffer, adding a dependency on the [Splat Depth Pass](1078-splat-depth-pass.md). The fragment shader logic is more complex than simple point sprites, potentially causing pipeline stalls if the depth buffer sampling is not cached efficiently [G].
-*   **When to Use:** Use when high-quality translucent rendering is required and the pipeline supports multi-pass rendering with G-Buffers. Avoid when hardware resources are extremely limited and a single-pass solution is mandatory [G].
